WEBVTT
1
00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:07.110
Hello and welcome to our course,
Functions, Tools and Agents with LanChain.

2
00:00:07.690 --> 00:00:10.150
LLMs have demonstrated
an amazing ability to

3
00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.410
interact with humans
using natural language,

4
00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.890
which has opened the door
to many new applications.

5
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:19.910
But how can LLMs also interact with
our existing software infrastructure?

6
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.350
For example, by letting
it decide when to make

7
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.140
a function call to a
different program to get

8
00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:25.530
more information call
to a different program

9
00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.309
to get more information
or to take an action.

10
00:00:28.309 --> 00:00:31.480
LLMs were originally designed
to generate text for humans,

11
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.340
but some LLMs have now been
trained to output formatted data,

12
00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.450
such as values stored as JSON,

13
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:43.429
to make it easy to let the LLM decide
when to call other code as a subroutine.

14
00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:47.369
This significantly expands
what you can do with LMs,

15
00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.329
such as let them extract
information from structured or

16
00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:54.710
tabular data which LMs have
typically struggled with.

17
00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:58.289
Here to tell you more
about this is Harrison Chase,

18
00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:02.450
co-founder and CEO of Landchain, who has
also taught two previous short courses.

19
00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:03.729
Welcome back, Harrison.

20
00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:05.950
Thanks, Andrew. So great to be back.

21
00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.069
You're right, this new capability
which OpenAI has named function

22
00:01:13.969 --> 00:01:16.810
Harrison, you've described
Langchain in other courses,

23
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.129
but maybe you could
describe what has changed

24
00:01:19.129 --> 00:01:21.129
and what you'd be covering in this course.

25
00:01:21.569 --> 00:01:21.989
Absolutely.

26
00:01:22.549 --> 00:01:26.450
As you know, Langchain is an open
source library that helps developers

27
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.049
bridge the gap between
traditional software and LLMs.

28
00:01:29.769 --> 00:01:33.689
It allows developers to support
any number of different LLMs and

29
00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:36.549
provides over 500 integrations
to different language models,

30
00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:40.829
vector stores, and tools, as well as
supporting memory chains and agents.

31
00:01:41.849 --> 00:01:44.810
There are two significant changes that
you will be getting into during the course.

32
00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.510
The second is changes to take advantage
of the new function calling capability.

33
00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:01.469
You'll learn how to use that
directly and we'll also show how

34
00:02:01.469 --> 00:02:04.230
it can be used to do tasks
like tagging or extracting data.

35
00:02:05.189 --> 00:02:09.150
Function Calling makes building tools
for LLMs simpler and more reliable.

36
00:02:09.870 --> 00:02:10.289
You'll build some tools, and then, use
them to build a conversational agent.

37
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.389
You get to use all of those elements
in the course and in the final project.

38
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:24.689
That sounds great, Harrison. And I think,

39
00:02:32.169 --> 00:02:35.009
Many people have worked to
make this course possible.

40
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.870
We're grateful to Lance Martin
and Nuno Kampas from Landchain.

41
00:02:40.729 --> 00:02:42.439
And on the deeplearning.ai side,

42
00:02:42.439 --> 00:02:45.849
Jeff Lodwick and Eshmel Gagari
also contributed to this course.